Vim(), downloadable charityware from

http://www.vim.org

is programmable in the very strong, scripting sense,
with a Turing-complete scripting language very
easily read by experienced programmers, but since it
also supports keystroke capturing to macros (one
named for each letter of the alphabet), and those
macros can call one another, and you can pop the
macros from their containers into text in a file and
save that file, then later recapture them into the
same or differntly named macro containers, sheer
laziness has kept me from ever bothering to learn
the much stronger scripting language, but I can make
the keystroke macro language fix dinner and clean
house.  The macro language is every bit as arcane as
emacs()' command language, though.

Vim() also has a splendid integrated help package,
accessible through the same split windows mode you
use to edit text.

Unlike emacs(), though, vim() is modal, which can
take a lot of learning curve to stop cursing.  I've
been using vim() and several of its predecessors /
siblings since 1984, and I still get caught typing
stuff meant for text entry mode in commands mode.

Vim() does support syntax highlighting, and has a
wonderful assortment of prebuilt ones for many of
the usual programming languages, but also allows
you to roll-your-own if you care to do so. The
syntax highlighting keys off the file name tag,
which has its plusses and minuses; IIRC, you can
override vim()'s guesses on that issue.

Vim() will happily read documents in DOS mode and
write them in Unix mode, or vice versa. I suspect
it also translates Mac-mode line terminators to
the other flavors, but I've never tried that.

For a NetHack player who uses the home row keys
to navigate, vim() is a delight, since the
navigation keys for NetHack were adopted from
vim()'s predecessor, vi(). Thus, you may already
know the rudiments of getting around in a document.

Emacs() was designed for programmers by programmers,
I suppose, so it assumes that the user _is_ a
programmer, and that doing things "programmer style"
is a reasonable way of operating.

Vi(), and thus vim(), was originally oriented at
secretaries, instead, and so has more ways to pop
easily to various flavors of logical breakpoints in
the document (word, line, sentence, paragraph,
section, ...; beginning or end of each) than you
will probably ever learn. The "next paragraph" and
"next word" ones you'll use every few seconds,
though.  Most of the programmer-oriented
enhancements to vim() came as _additions_ to this
text-as-text users' orientation, so vim() is still
profoundly easier to use as a navigation tool than
is emacs().

Vim() is smart about comment delimiters in source
code, too, so that it helps you reformat blocks of
comments while reinserting the comment delimiters on
your behalf, a great time saver.

Vim() is ported to pretty much everything with a
market share, so just like micro-emacs(), you can
use it on almost every platform where you work or
play.

xanthian, in lust for a text editor, obviously.

Compared to its predecessor, vi(), the memory
footprint of vim() has gotten pretty porked up,
maybe sixfold, but it is nowhere close to the size
of emacs(), in case that is an issue for you.

Don't continue your fear of emacs() forever, though.
It is a wonderful and powerful tool once you get
past terror of its sheer logical size. I put off
learning it for five years after I started using
Unix systems, including vi(), and once I'd learned
emacs(), I wanted to kick my former self for the
delay and all the time I'd spent working without it.

I just prefer vim() for most text entry chores, now.



